 <u> SUMMARY </u> :  Establishes the Public School Performance  
Accountability Program that would apply to all schools,  
including charter schools, consisting of a state Academic  
Performance Index (API), an Immediate  
Intervention/Underperforming Schools Program (IIUSP), and a High  
Achieving/Improving Schools Program (HAISP).  Specifically, <u> this  
bill: </u> 

ACADEMIC PERFORMANCE INDEX (API)

1)Requires that by July 1, 1999, the Superintendent of Public  
  Instruction (SPI) develop and the State Board of Education  
  (SBE), with the help of an advisory committee, approve an API  
  to be used to measure performance of schools, especially the  
  academic performance of pupils, and demonstrate comparable  
  improvement in academic achievement by all numerically  
  significant ethnic and socioeconomically disadvantaged  
  subgroups within schools.  API shall consist of a variety of  
  indicators currently reported to the State Department of  
  Education (SDE) including, but not limited to, the results of  








                                                         <u> SB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  2

  the Standardized Testing and Reporting (STAR) achievement  
  test, attendance rates for pupils and certificated school  
  personnel for elementary schools, middle schools, and  
  secondary schools, and the graduation rates for pupils in  
  secondary schools.  Only the test scores of pupils enrolled in  
  a school district for one year or more will be reported in  
  API.  At least 60% of the value of API shall be from tests  
  including STAR, the new high school exit exam if enacted by SB  
  2x (O'Connell), pending in the Assembly, and the yet to be  
  developed matrix exam when these tests are found to be  
  reliable for this purpose.  The data would be disaggregated by  
  special education status, English language learners (ELL),  
  socioeconomic status, gender, and ethnic groups.  API shall be  
  developed for comprehensive high schools, middle schools, and  
  elementary schools.  The accountability system for schools  
  with less than 100 students and for schools under the  
  jurisdiction of a county board of education or a county  
  superintendent of schools, community day schools, and  
  alternative schools, including continuation high schools and  
  independent study schools, will be deferred until July 1, 2000  
  so that SBE may develop an alternative approach.

2)Provides that SPI and SBE must identify growth targets for all  
  schools based on their API baseline score as measured in July  
  1999.  The growth targets:  a) must have a minimum of 5%  
  growth annually; b) must have differential growth based on  
  grade level; and, c) may be higher for the lowest performing  
  schools because they have the greatest room for improvement.   
  In addition, API performance subgroups must demonstrate  
  comparable improvements in academic achievement by all  
  numerically significant ethnic and socioeconomically  
  disadvantaged subgroups within schools, as defined.

3)Provides that an API performance target that represents the  
  proficiency level required to meet state academic content  
  standards must be adopted by SPI and SBE once the State  
  Performance Standards have been identified.  Schools may  
  either meet the state standards or meet their growth targets  
  to be eligible for a Governor's Performance Award Program  
  (GPAP).

4)Provides that by June 2000 every school shall be ranked in  
  decile categories by grade level (i.e., elementary, middle,  
  and high school) and, by June 2001, the rankings will indicate  
  the target annual growth rates along with the actual growth  








                                                         <u> SB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  3

  rates attained by the schools.  These will be published by SPI  
  on the Internet.  Schools will be required to report their  
  rankings in their School Accountability Report Card (i.e., as  
  provided for under Proposition 98) and the school board of  
  each district will be required to hold a public hearing to  
  discuss the results of the annual ranking of its schools.  API  
  will also be used to identify underperforming schools; those  
  which fail to meet their annual growth target.

5)Provides that SPI shall establish a broadly representative and  
  diverse advisory committee to advise on all appropriate  
  matters relative to the creation of API and the implementation  
  of IIUSP and HAISP.  Members of the advisory committee shall  
  serve without compensation for terms not to exceed two years.   
  SDE shall provide staff to the advisory committee.

IMMEDIATE INTERVENTION/UNDERPERFORMING SCHOOL PROGRAM (IIUSP)

1)Establishes IIUSP to begin by August 15, 1999 when schools  
  that performed below 50% on the STAR testing program, both in  
  the spring of 1998 and 1999, will be invited to participate in  
  the program.  By September 1, 1999, SPI will notify 430  
  selected participants.  Schools will be selected by decile  
  rank (e.g., 10% ranges up to 50%) not to exceed 86 per decile.  
   Of the 430 schools, there would be no more than 301  
  elementary, 78 middle, and 52 high schools that represent  
  statewide proportionate geographic representation of urban and  
  rural schools.

2)Provides that if the total number of voluntary participants  
  does not reach 430, schools will be selected by SPI to fill  
  out the 430th number in a random selection process approved by  
  SBE.  A school required to participate may take any action to  
  improve pupil performance at that school if the action is not  
  otherwise prohibited by state or federal law and does not  
  require reimbursement by the Commission on State Mandates.

3)Provides that each of the 430 schools selected before  
  September 1, 1999, will be awarded a planning grant of $50,000  
  as determined by criteria to be developed by SPI and approved  
  by SBE.  Each selected participant must by October 1, contract  
  with an external evaluator and form a broad-based school site  
  and community team for development of an action plan by the  
  following March 15th. The evaluators will come from an  
  approved list developed by SPI and approved by SBE.  The list  








                                                         <u> SB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  4

  may include the private sector experts as well as institutions  
  of higher education, county offices of education and  
  educational consortia, and must meet certain criteria as  
  specified in the bill.  By April 15, the governing board must  
  submit the plan to SPI with a request for funding.  By May 15,  
  SPI must recommend and SBE must act on the local funding  
  requests.

4)Provides that SBE may waive all code sections or any part of  
  code sections, or any regulations controlling specified  
  education programs.

5)Provides that implementation is phased in over two years  
  beginning in the year 2000.  In the first phase, the year  
  2000-2001, grants of up to $200 per pupil enrolled in the  
  school (i.e., with a minimum of $50,000 per school site) are  
  provided if the school's application is approved before June  
  15.  The district must match this amount of funding from  
  existing resources and is allowed maximum flexibility in the  
  expenditure of any new or existing categorical funds not  
  otherwise prohibited by state or federal law and shall  
  redirect for the purposes of their academic improvement plan  
  new or existing categorical or general purpose funds.  At the  
  end of the first year, if the school fails to meet its  
  short-term growth, the local school board, upon consultation,  
  can impose consequences, including reassignment of school  
  personnel, negotiation of site-specific amendments to  
  collective bargaining agreements, or other changes deemed  
  appropriate, in order to continue implementing the action  
  plan, and to make progress toward meeting the school's growth  
  targets.

6)Provides that at the end of the second year of implementation  
  (i.e., 2001-2002) the program provides rewards for a school  
  that meets or exceeds its growth target each year for the two  
  years.  Funds received from this program shall be used at the  
  school's discretion.  A school that has not met its  
  performance goals but demonstrates significant growth is  
  allowed to continue to participate in the program for an  
  additional year and to receive funding.

7)Provides that a school that does not meet its performance  
  goals and has failed to show significant growth after two  
  years in the program will be deemed a low performing school  
  and SPI will then assume all of the legal rights, duties, and  








                                                         <u> SB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  5

  powers of the local school board with respect to that school.   
  The principal will be reassigned, subject to a specified  
  process, and SPI must do at least one of the following:

   a)   Revise attendance options for pupils to allow them to  
     attend any public school in which space is available;

   b)   Allow the creation of a charter school by parents at the  
     existing school site;

   c)   Reassign other certificated employees of the school;

   d)   Renegotiate a new collective bargaining agreement at the  
     expiration of the existing agreement;

   e)   Reorganize the school;

   f)   Close the school; or,

   g)   Assign, under the supervision of SPI, the management of  
     the school to a college, university, county office of  
     education, or other appropriate educational institution.   
     However, SPI may not assume the management of the school.

8)Provides that in addition, SPI, in consultation with SBE, may  
  take other actions against the school district and district  
  governing board including appointment of a new superintendent  
  or suspension of the authority of the governing board with  
  respect to the schools that are low performing.

9)Provides a total of $96.15 million (i.e., state funding of  
  $63.704 million and $32.446 million in federal funds) for  
  planning and implementation grants for IIUSP.

THE HIGH ACHIEVING/IMPROVING SCHOOLS PROGRAM (HAISP)

1)Establishes HAISP, under which monetary and non-monetary  
  awards will be granted pursuant to GPAP to schools that meet  
  or exceed API performance growth targets, and demonstrate  
  comparable improvement in academic achievement by all  
  numerically significant ethnic and socioeconomically  
  disadvantaged subgroups within schools. Commencing in June  
  2000, and every June thereafter, SPI, with approval of SBE,  
  shall rank all public schools based on the API.  The schools  
  shall be ranked by grade level of instruction provided and  








                                                         <u> SB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  6

  shall include three categories:  a) elementary; b) middle;  
  and,  c) high school.  Commencing in June 2001, the rankings  
  shall indicate the target annual growth rates of schools, the  
  actual growth rates attained by the schools, and how growth  
  rates compare schools that have similar characteristics.   
  Similar characteristics include, but are not limited to, the  
  following characteristics, insofar as data is available from  
  SDE data:  a) pupil mobility; b) pupil ethnicity; c) pupil  
  socioeconomic status; d) percentage of teachers who are fully  
  credentialed; e) percentage of teachers who hold emergency  
  credentials; f) percentage of pupils who are English language  
  learners; g) average class size per grade level; and , h)  
  whether the schools operate multitrack year round educational  
  programs.  SPI is to annually publish these rankings on the  
  Internet.

2)Provides that the criteria for the disbursement of the  
  monetary awards will be developed by SPI and approved by SBE.   
  The monetary awards will be available on a per-pupil or  
  per-school basis and will not exceed $150 per pupil enrolled  
  subject to funds appropriated in the annual budget act.  All  
  schools, including schools participating in IIUSP , are  
  eligible to participate in GPAP.

3)Provides that there would also be a range of non-monetary  
  awards.  Schools that are eligible for performance awards may  
  request that SBE waive all code sections or any part of code  
  sections, or any regulations controlling specified education  
  programs.

4)Provides $96.15 million from the General Fund (GF) for HAISP  
  and in addition, $900,000 is appropriated from GF to SPI to  
  administer the overall accountability program.

PROGRAM EVALUATION

1)Provides that by January 31, 2002, each school district with  
  schools participating in IIUSP shall submit to SPI an  
  evaluation of the impact, costs, and benefits of the program  
  as it relates to the school district and the schools under its  
  jurisdiction that are participating in the program and whether  
  or not the schools met their growth targets, with an analysis  
  of the reasons why the schools have or have not met those  
  growth targets.









                                                         <u> SB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  7

2)Provides that by January 15, 2000, SPI shall develop, and SBE  
  shall approve, the guidelines for a request for proposal for  
  an independent evaluation.  By September 1, 2000, SPI shall  
  contract with an independent evaluator to prepare a  
  comprehensive evaluation of the implementation, impact, costs,  
  and benefits of IIUSP and HAISP.

 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :   According to the Assembly Appropriations  
Committee:

1)General Fund (GF) (Proposition 98) costs of $160 million  
  annually, beginning in Fiscal Year (FY) 1999-2000, by  
  appropriating $63.85 million for IIUSP and $96.15 million for  
  HAISP.

2)Federal fund costs of $32.3 million annually for IIUSP.  These  
  funds are proposed in the Governor's budget. 

3)GF (Non-Proposition 98) costs of $3 million annually beginning  
  in FY 1999-2000 to the California Department of Education to  
  collect, analyze, and report data for API, and to implement  
  IIUSP and HAISP.

 <u> COMMENTS </u> :  This bill is one of four bills sponsored by the  
Governor as part of his call for an extraordinary session of the  
Legislature dedicated to education reform issues.  The other  
three bills address:  a high school exit exam [SB 2X  
(O'Connell), pending in the Assembly], peer assistance and  
review for teachers [AB 1X (Villaraigosa), pending in the  
Senate], and reading reform [AB 2X Mazzoni), pending in the  
Senate].


